    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d660">

                <pb facs="0643" n="639" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg639"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/473: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-01-31T16:00:00+09:00">January 31, 1938—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 31—6:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>59. Department’s 31, January 30, 1 p.m., integrity of Chinese customs. My British
                    colleague received his instructions today and is prepared <pb facs="0644"
                        n="640" xml:id="pg640"/> to act. I have therefore today presented to the
                    Japanese Government a note conforming precisely to the Department’s text.<anchor
                        n="43" xml:id="d660-f1"/></p>
                <p>Repeated to Shanghai for repetition to Hankow and Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d660-f1" n="43" xml:id="d660-f1-content">
                <p>See <ref target="frus1931-41v01#pg_738"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >i</hi>, p. 738</ref>; the Department on January 31 also replied to the
                    British Embassy’s inquiry on this subject. For reply of the Japanese Foreign
                    Office, on March 22, see <ref target="frus1931-41v01#pg_742"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, p. 742</ref>; it replied likewise to
                    British and French representations.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
